[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Distributed Transactions & Two-Phase Commit (2PC)"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In modern distributed system design, ensuring data consistency across multiple independent databases or services is a primary challenge. When a single business operation requires updates to multiple databases, a traditional ACID transaction is insufficient because local database locks do not span network boundaries."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"The Challenge of Distributed Data"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Consider an e-commerce checkout flow where a user purchases an item. This action requires deducting inventory from the Warehouse Service, charging the user via the Payment Service, and creating an order in the Order Service. If the payment succeeds but the inventory update fails, the system enters an inconsistent state. Distributed transactions aim to solve this by providing atomicity across networks."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"How Two-Phase Commit (2PC) Works"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Two-Phase Commit (2PC) protocol is a classic atomic commitment protocol used to ensure that all participating databases in a distributed transaction either commit or roll back together. It operates in two distinct phases coordinated by a transaction coordinator."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 1 (Prepare): The coordinator asks all participant nodes if they are ready to commit. Each node executes the transaction up to the point of committing, writes entries to a local log, and replies with a vote (VOTE_COMMIT or VOTE_ABORT)."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Phase 2 (Commit/Abort): If all participants vote YES, the coordinator sends a global commit command. If any participant votes NO or fails to respond, the coordinator sends a global abort command, forcing all nodes to roll back."},{"order":9,"type":"subheading","text":"Pseudo-Code Implementation Example"},{"order":10,"type":"code","text":"function execute2PC(transaction) {
    let participants = getParticipants(transaction);
    let votes = [];

    // Phase 1: Prepare
    for (let p of participants) {
        let vote = p.sendPrepare(transaction);
        votes.push(vote);
    }

    // Decision
    if (votes.every(v => v === 'VOTE_COMMIT')) {
        for (let p of participants) {
            p.sendCommit(transaction);
        }
        return 'SUCCESS';
    } else {
        for (let p of participants) {
            p.sendAbort(transaction);
        }
        return 'ABORTED';
    }
}"},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawbacks and Limitations of 2PC"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"While 2PC guarantees strong consistency, it has severe limitations in large-scale distributed systems, particularly regarding performance and availability."},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Blocking Nature: During Phase 1 and Phase 2, participant nodes hold locks on data resources. If the coordinator crashes permanently after Phase 1, participants remain blocked indefinitely, unable to release locks."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Latency: 2PC requires multiple network round trips and synchronous blocking calls, which drastically reduces throughput and increases tail latencies."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Single Point of Failure: The transaction coordinator is a critical bottleneck and failure point, requiring high availability configurations."},{"order":16,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion & Alternatives"},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"Due to the availability and performance bottlenecks of 2PC (which violates the CAP theorem for availability-focused systems), modern architectures often prefer eventual consistency patterns. Techniques like the Saga Pattern, which uses compensating transactions instead of locking resources, are heavily favored in microservices over traditional 2PC."}]
